In article <1991Feb25.232600.16817@rodan.acs.syr.edu> edelsohn@sccs.syr.edu (David Edelsohn) writes:
>	My question is how long will it take DEC to incorporate X11R5 (shipping
>to X Consortium members at the end of this month) into their product line?
>Public release of X11R5 is rumored to occur during a much warmer season this
>year.  Again the timing is great for DEC as they convert DECWindows's GUI from
>XUI to OSF/Motif.  Given DEC's lead time and product testing, I guess that it
>will ship with Ultrix 5.1 next calendar year, but I would like to be
>pleasantly surprised.

Hopefully, not as long as R4 has (now in field test).  But note that any 
rumored alpha release of R5 is a long way from final release, much less any
vendor's product release.  If you are a consortium member, you know that
MIT alpha and beta test releases are 1) private affairs, and 2) cannot be
shipped as product to customers.  The only public statement about R5 at this
date has been to expect it once the weather in Boston has gone from cold 
(right now) to warm, to hot...

I would, as an educated guess, that you'll
see Motif before R5, but then again, I'm not in a product group.

To give you a better way to guage such things, recognise any MIT release
must be in hand at least long enough to do any additional work and testing
in advance of a product field test; field tests are typically three months
long.  So there is at best a 4-5 month lag from an MIT release to a product
release, and that presumes precise synchronization with internal release
schedules, and no software base on top to worry about.  In the R4 case, 
it was EXACTLY wrong for us; R4 was available
a month or two late for the DS5000 with all of its display types.  And the
larger your internal software base (in Digital's case, we now ship a
large pile of software on top) the longer it takes, due to testing.

So for commercial vendors, I would guess it typically takes 6-9 months to get
a release out; when things synchronize wrong, it can easily take a bit
over a year.
				- Jim
--
Digital Equipment Corporation
Cambridge Research Laboratory


