In article <4983@umbc3.UMBC.EDU>, paulr@umbc5.umbc.edu (Paul Riddle) writes:
> Normally in this case we would just have field service come out and
> replace the drive; however, this machine isn't covered by our service
> contract, so I would like to see if I can salvage the disk.  Is there
> any way I can do a low level format on an RD53 so it can find its bad
> blocks?  This would seem like the best solution.  If it's not
> possible, is there anything else I can do, or should I forget it?

  I believe you've encountered a known problem. The RD53 drive is more
sensitive to induced noise than other drives. DEC issued a Field Change
Order which replaces the 50-pin ribbon cable between the RQDXn control-
ler and the BA23's signal distribution board with a shielded version.

  The short form description of the FCO is "RD53 loss of format - need
to reformat drive every few months" or something similar. I can get you
the exact FCO number if you need it. It'd be cheaper to buy the FCO kit
and install it yourself (if you're up to it) than having DEC do it. On
the other hand, see below for formatting troubles:

  Once you've swapped the cable you'll need to reformat the drive. This
is harder than it needs to be on a MicroVAX, since you will need the DEC
"Maintenance Diagnostics" as opposed to the "Customer Diagnostics" to
successfully reformat the drive (since the customer version can't handle
an unformatted drive, which is what your RD53 will look like to it). De-
pending on your controller, you have various choices. The easiest thing
is probably to have DEC do it for you (when they swap the cable), but it
will cost you. Someone with the same controller could reformat the drive
for you, but beware: you need the same ROM version on your controller as
on the one that formatted the drive - if the formatting controller had
newer firmware than your controller, yours will refuse to see the drive.

  On the other hand, your drive could just be kaput. No way to know with-
out trying to format it.

