In article <4983@umbc3.UMBC.EDU>, paulr@umbc5.umbc.edu (Paul Riddle) writes:
> 
> Hi everyone,
> 
> I've got a MicroVAX II here that was donated to our department.
> [ Stuff deleted ]
> I ran through it with the radisk utility and got a bunch of force
> error modifiers set, and now it refuses to fsck, complaining that it
> can't read these blocks.

	When a block gets replaced by radisk or during "normal"
	operation one of the two things will happen to it; the
	BBR algorithms will manange to salvage a good copy of
	the data or they won't.  If it manages to get a good
	copy of the data to write into the replaced then all
	continues as before, except perhaps for a little per-
	formance loss.  However, if it can't get a good copy of
	the data it marks the sector as having a "forced error"
	(FE).  Most host interfaces just treat this as an I/O
	error and depending on the application, die or perhaps
	try to do something about it.

	What you can do about the FEs is to make note of the sectors 
	that have been corrupted and use the -c flag of radisk to 
	clear the flag.  Then wander through the file system with 
	fsck(8), icheck(8) and ncheck(8) repairing the damage as
	best you can.

>  I got around this earlier by newfs'ing the disk, but the problem 
> has returned.

	Another way to clear an FE is to overwrite the block with
	new data.  That's why newfs(8) would sometimes clear them;
	it didn't read the particular blocks first and wrote new
	data.
	
> Normally in this case we would just have field service come out and
> replace the drive; however, this machine isn't covered by our service
> contract, so I would like to see if I can salvage the disk.  Is there
> any way I can do a low level format on an RD53 so it can find its bad
> blocks?  

	If you have the customer diagnostics the formatter is
	included.  If you don't have them check with your local
	DEC office to see if they can get you a copy.

> This would seem like the best solution.  If it's not possible, is 
> there anything else I can do, or should I forget it?

	Depends on the severity of the problem.  If multiple passes
	of radisk(8) reveal differents sets of bad blocks, then you
	may have a seriously sick disk and it might not be worthwhile
	to mask the problem for a short time.

	If all fails try a simple cost analysis; how much time do you 
	spend repairing, reformatting, restoreing files, etc.  How much 
	does that cost in your time spent?  How much does it cost in the
	time other people loose for not being able to use a system?  How
	much does a replacement disk cost?
> 
> Paul Riddle                         | paulr@umbc3.umbc.edu

-- 
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxn.dec.com


